course: dictionary
lesson: 1116
title: A tree that named a country
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: Some words name a thing you can hold or see, like brazilwood. Brazilwood is a tropical tree from the coast of Brazil with hard red wood. When you meet a new word, look at the words around it for clues: «Portugal colonised Brazil from the 1530s, at first for brazilwood, a red dye wood that gave the land its name.» The words after the comma tell you what brazilwood is: a red dye wood.
explain: Brazilwood is a noun, so it names something. You can ask: is it a tree, or is it wood? It is both. The living tree is a brazilwood tree, and the red wood from it can be called brazilwood too. In «brazilwood bow», the word brazilwood tells you what kind of bow it is.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. If you read «a red dye wood that gave the land its name» and think brazilwood means a red dye wood, the sentence still makes sense. That is a good sign your meaning is right.
fact: Brazilwood means a tropical tree from the coast of Brazil with hard red wood.
fact: Brazilwood also means the red wood of this tree, used to make red dye and the best bows for violins.
fact: Brazilwood gave the country Brazil its name, because Portuguese traders came for its red dye.
fact: Brazilwood is now endangered, which means it is at risk of dying out.
fact: You can say «brazilwood tree» for the living tree, and «brazilwood bow» for a bow made from its wood.
exercise: «Portugal colonised Brazil, at first for brazilwood, a red dye wood.» What is brazilwood in this sentence? | A type of ship | A kind of sugar | *A red dye wood | A river in Portugal
exercise: Musicians use brazilwood to make what? | Piano keys | *Fine violin bows | Drum skins | Guitar strings
exercise: In «brazilwood bow», which word tells you what kind of bow it is? | kind | bow | *brazilwood | the
test: Why did Portuguese traders come to Brazil in the lesson's sentence? | *For its red dye | To grow tea on the coast | To build castles in the hills | To sell violins in Lisbon
test: «Brazilwood is now endangered.» What does endangered mean? | Very large and strong | Full of red dye | Hidden under the ground | *At risk of dying out
exam: Which statement about brazilwood is true? | It is a kind of fish that lives in the sea | It is a metal used to make ships | *It is a tropical tree from the coast of Brazil with hard red wood | It is a sugar plantation in Portugal
